<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Jämtland</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1178 CE (approx.) · capital Frösön</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing farming province of central Scandinavia, ruled by its own assembly on Frösön until King Sverre brought it under Norway in 1178.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jämtland, around the great lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storsjon">Storsjön</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, was settled by farmers who, according to tradition, governed themselves without a king. The sagas describe settlers from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and the region lay between the two kingdoms.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Jamtamót, the province's assembly, met on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/froson">Frösön</a> and decided law and justice. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/froso-runestone">Frösö runestone</a>, the northernmost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> runestone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostman-gudfastsson">Östman Gudfastsson</a> had Jämtland Christianised in the 11th century. The economy rested on farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, hunting and the fur and iron trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1178 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sverre-of-norway">Sverre of Norway</a> defeated the Jämts and made them pay tax, and Jämtland became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>, though it belonged to the Swedish archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bromsebro-1645">Treaty of Brömsebro</a> in 1645, and the Jamtamót tradition is still a point of regional pride.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jamtland.jpg" alt="Jämtland" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%A4mtland_T%C3%A4nnforsen_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1178 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Frösön</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jamtland, Jamtamót, Republic of Jämtland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1039&amp;focus=jamtland" aria-label="Jämtland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4973 83 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jämtland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5293 339L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 339h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 339h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1039&amp;focus=jamtland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

